  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The most probable outcome of Lottery A is 1000 Euros with a 50% probability, and the other outcome is 0 Euros with 50% probability. Since both outcomes in Lottery A have equal probabilities (50%), the most probable outcome is not unique, but the maximum probability is 50%. For Lottery B, the most probable outcome is 500 Euros with a 100% probability, which is clearly the highest probability outcome. The decision reason compares the most probable outcomes based on favorability (i.e., monetary value) when their probabilities are the highest. Here, Lottery B has a higher probability (100% vs 50%), so its most probable outcome is more certain. However, the reason focuses on which most probable outcome is more favorable (higher value) when probabilities are equal or when considering only the highest-probability outcome per lottery. In this case, Lottery A’s most probable outcome (1000 Euros, at 50%) has a higher value than Lottery B’s most probable outcome (500 Euros, at 100%). Thus, the reason would prefer Lottery A if it only compares the values of the most probable outcomes, regardless of the probability level. However, if the reason requires comparing outcomes with strictly the highest probability (i.e., 100% vs 50%), then Lottery B’s outcome is uniquely most probable. The rule as stated emphasizes 'the most favorable outcome with the highest (maximum) probability' per lottery — so per lottery, we identify the outcome with the highest probability (in A: either 1000 or 0, both 50%; we may take the more favorable one, 1000; in B: 500 at 100%). Then compare: 1000 vs 500. Since 1000 > 500, the reason would prefer Lottery A. Therefore, the reason can be applied and would prefer Lottery A.",
